Objective: This 21 day email course is designed for the student to gain
a clear understanding of the credit repair process so that they can
approach the client file and create an action plan with certainty. The
content of this course is also designed to help the student effectively
communicate credit information so that as a business owner they can feel
comfortable and confident when speaking about the subject of credit to
leads, clients, and the general public. *The information contained in
this course is not legal advice and should not be taken as such. The
information presented in this lesson is for educational and information
purposes only. If you need legal advice, please seek council*

Bonus Lesson #6:
Disputing Bankruptcies

You ever wonder how the credit bureaus obtain the public record
information you find on the consumer report? If you've learned
anything at all about the bureaus throughout this course it's probably
that their systems are automated. So, certainly they don't have a
staff of people searching this information. In this lesson we will take
a look at public records, specifically bankruptcies to get a clear
understanding of the Who's reporting them, Who's furnishing the
information, Who's not Verifying them, Where the information actually
comes from, When to dispute with Who and How to use this information to
assist you in your disputing efforts. 

Bankruptcy Basics

There are different types of bankruptcies, but for the purpose of this
lesson we will specifically focus on the two types of Bankruptcies filed
by individuals: Chapter 7 and Chapter 13.

Chapter 7 bankruptcy, sometimes called a straight bankruptcy, is a
liquidation proceeding. When you file a chapter 7 bankruptcy petition,
you give consent for the trustee to take possession of your assets and
sell them to raise cash to satisfy your debts. If any debts remain
unpaid because sufficient assets aren't available, these balances are
discharged, and you're relieved of responsibility for paying them.
Bankruptcy law does allow debtors to keep a certain amount of property
after going through bankruptcy proceedings. This is called "exempt"
property and it is exempt from the bankruptcy estate. Property that
cannot be exempted is, appropriately, called "non-exempt" property. In a
chapter 7, after all property non-exempt property is liquidated to pay
debtors, the debtor receives a discharge of all dischargeable debts
usually within four months. In the vast majority of cases the debtor has
no assets that they would lose so Chapter 7 will give that person a
relatively quick "fresh start". 

Assets that can be liquidated include: personal property, pets and
animals, real estate, some retirement benefits, life insurance,
inheritances, interest in a business and intellectual property rights
such as patents. If there is a pending lawsuit, the bankruptcy trustee
is entitled to the proceeds in many cases.

Chapter 13 bankruptcy is more commonly known as a reorganization
bankruptcy.  Chapter 13 is filed by individuals who want to pay off
their debts over a period of three to five years; therefore, assets are
not at risk for liquidation.

This type of bankruptcy appeals to individuals who have non-exempt
property that they want to keep. It is also only an option for
individuals who have predictable income and whose income is sufficient
to pay their reasonable expenses with some amount left over to pay off
their debts. In this type of case, creditors are paid from the
petitioner's income that is left over each month after they pay their
necessary living expenses.

A liability is a debt you owe, or an obligation you have incurred. The
clearest examples of a liability are credit card debts, medical bills,
and personal loans. These liabilities are considered "unsecured,
non-priority debts." Taxes, child support, and alimony are also
liabilities, though they are considered "priority", and are not
discharged and therefore must be paid back in bankruptcy. Mortgage and
car payments are ongoing debt obligations that bankruptcy law allows the
debtor to continuing paying on and keep the property.

Once filed, even if the case is dismissed, the bankruptcy filing can
legally stay on the credit report for 10 years.

Bankruptcy Discharge & When It Occurs

A bankruptcy discharge releases the debtor from personal liability for
certain specified types of debts. The debtor is no longer legally
required to pay any debts that are discharged. The discharge is a
permanent order prohibiting the creditors of the debtor from taking any
form of collection action on discharged debts, including legal action
and communications with the debtor, such as telephone calls, letters,
and personal contacts. 

The timing of the discharge varies, depending on the chapter under which
the case is filed.

In a chapter 7, the court usually grants the discharge on the expiration
of the time fixed for filing a complaint objecting to discharge and the
time fixed for filing a motion to dismiss the case for substantial
abuse. Typically, this occurs about four months after the date the
debtor files the petition with the clerk of the bankruptcy court. 

In a chapter 13, the court generally grants the discharge as soon as the
debtor completes all payments under the plan. Since a chapter 13 plan
may provide for payments to be made over three to five years, the
discharge typically occurs about four years after the date of filing.
(U.S. Courts)

Dealing with Bankruptcies on the Credit Report

In the past, disputing a bankruptcy used to be what most considered a
fairly simple process. There were various strategies and methods that
worked, but the most common was verifying with the courts whether or not
the report or supply information to the bureaus. Even if you ask around
today, some will still offer up this outdated advice to assist you in
your disputing efforts. And while it may yield a desired result from
time to time, it's likely the BK would have as easily been removed
without applying any strategy.

Following the Court Verification Method you'd (1) dispute the BK with
the bureaus, requesting a re-investigation to verify the accuracy of the
listing. If the BK came back verified you'd (2) Send a letter to the
clerk of court reporting the bankruptcy asking about their method of
verification with the bureaus, with a stamped returned envelope for them
to respond back. The letter may have looked/sounded like this... 
RE: Case #

Dear Clerk of Court, 

I am inquiring about your method of verification with the credit
bureaus. I would also like to know the procedure you follow when
furnishing information reported to the bureaus. Will you please be so
kind and respond using the stamped envelope provided. 

Sincerely, 

Your Name

In response, the clerk would respond with a letter or form indicating
that they do not verify with nor furnish information to the credit
bureaus by mail, electronically, or by any other means. Today, you can
even find this disclaimer directly on some court sites.

(3) Your next step would be to make a copy of the clerk's response and
send it in to the bureaus with a letter letting them know you have proof
they Did Not verify anything with the courthouse and requesting
immediate deletion. 

The problem with the court verification method is, a few years ago the
bureaus used to list the courts as their source of information, even
though they never "actually" obtained their information from any court.
But this no longer the case. Today, they list the actual source of
information or Furnisher, Lexis Nexis. In a previous lesson we discussed
how Lexis Nexis is both a reporting agency and a furnisher. This
instance would be an example. The report information about consumers as
well as furnisher the data they house to other reporting agencies and
purchasers. Although Lexis Nexis isn't as automated as the bureaus,
they are not physically pulling and entering every single piece of data
for every public record that they report and/or sell. They use
applications that interact with other systems to pull information, such
as public records. The system that Lexis Nexis extracts their
information from is PACER (Public Access to Court Electronic Records).
PACER is an electronic public access service of United States federal
court documents. It allows users to obtain case and docket information
from the United States district courts, United States courts of appeals,
and United States bankruptcy courts (Wikipedia).

The applications and systems Lexis Nexis use to extract, transfer and
report the public record information isn't without error. This is
where your reasoning and factual disputing skills comes in. In fact,
much of the information regarding the bankruptcy, especially Chapter 13,
has no specific or designated field on the bureau reports and is
therefore not transferred from the furnisher of information to the
bureau. How logical and reasonable would it be to request verification
of information that you are certain a bureau doesn't have. Very! But
first you'll need to get access to this information to determine what
they have and what they do not as well as what's reporting correctly
and what isn't 

Knowing now that the bureaus are getting their public record information
from Lexis Nexis who is obtaining their information from PACER, you now
have the information you need to develop your plan of action. The first
thing that you would want to do is sign up for a PACER account at
pacer.gov. It's free; you only pay .10 per page for the pages you
view/print. Each time I teach a lesson on Bankruptcies I have to answer
this question, so I'll save myself the trouble right now. "No." "No."
and "No." You cannot dispute with PACER. It is an electronic system. BUT
understanding that, if there were ever any legal proceedings regarding
the reporting of a bankruptcy on a consumer file for which information
was obtained from PACER, being able to require PACER to testify to the
validity of its information would be rather funny...Or would it? "Your
honor, I'd like to call PACER to the stand." Okay...corny, I know, but
I hope you caught that little nugget*wink*

So, let's get to a sample Bankruptcy Dispute Flow...

Step 1: Order the Lexis Nexis Report. Why? Because that's where the
information comes from and you'd want to tackle the reporting directly
from the source (furnisher & reporting agency) before going to the
bureaus.

Step 2: Locate the public record in PACER. Once inside, you will find
that PACER has a ton of information. I suggest taking your time to get
familiar with the system so that you learn to easily locate case records
and documents. Using a client with a bankruptcy, locate their
information inside of PACER and review the documents to learn what type
of information is provided to you.

Step 3: Send a letter to verify the account with Lexis Nexis
If the account Remains...

Step 4: Use the discrepancies from the PACER Documents and compare them
to the Lexis Nexis Report. You are looking for missing and inaccurate
data. Factually dispute the BK with Lexis Nexis until it is deleted.

Step 5: Option 1 - Request verification with the bureaus; if the
account is verified, submit evidence of the deletion from Lexis Nexis
and question how they verified something that doesn't exist. Inform
them that it is no way possible they could have obtained verification of
accuracy from the furnisher when the furnisher was unable to verify the
information. 
Option 2 - Immediately send a copy of the deletion records from Lexis
Nexis with a letter to the Bureau informing them that the furnisher of
their information was unable to verify the accuracy of the account so
they also must immediately delete the information. 
Another Method: (1) Dispute all accounts listed in bankruptcy. Once
every account listed in bankruptcy has been removed, due to inaccurate
and/or incomplete reporting (because there are sure to be reporting
errors and you can find them within the files of PACER or just challenge
the reporting data), (2) dispute the bankruptcy with the reporting
agencies with a reason that looks/sounds something like, "What
Bankruptcy? If a bankruptcy had occurred, wouldn't I have had
creditors and debt included in the bankruptcy? It is not possible and
makes absolutely no sense to have a bankruptcy reporting when you
CLEARLY see by the items reporting on my credit report that there are no
accounts to have been included in this alleged bankruptcy? Maybe you
have my credit file confused with someone else. Immediately delete this
inaccurate information from my credit report." 

Bruce, I've provided you with three methods. One outdated, but may
still be effective from time to time and with certain reporting
agencies. One that requires a bit of research and analysis, but it may
give you a ton of evidence for factual disputing. The last one is a
removal tactic using what you already know to dispute accounts and then
go after the bankruptcy. There is more than one way to skin a cat. This
holds true to disputing or challenging all account types and information
on the consumer report. If you try a method that doesn't prove it self
successful with a particular account for a specific client, remember all
reports and situations are different. Keep positive and try another
method to reach your goal. Build a strategy box full of methods to
choose from and use in any situation. If that sounds like something you
could use then be on the look out because I just may have something
coming available very soon! 

